      "text": "In every instance, the periods are described by a triple consisting of $n$ and $m$. Square brackets are used to denote these tripels, for instance $\\{ 3,5 \\}$ would depict a division of 21 periods of 5 seconds.\nTo form one of these triples, it is necessary to pick $n$ numbers from the set $\\{ 1,2,...,60 \\}$ .\nDividing all possibilities by 2 due to counting trivial permutations is allowed by symmetry.\n$$\\frac{60 \\cdot 59 \\cdot 58 \\cdots 41}{2} = \\frac{60!}{41! \\cdot 2^{20}}$$\nA probability questions.  Problem # 1:\n\nA day can be evenly divided into 86,400 periods of 1 second; 43,200 periods of each 2 seconds; or in many other ways. In total, how many ways are there to divide a day into $n$ periods of $m$ seconds, where $n$ and $m$ are positive integers?\n\nThanks,\nMoeira\nis $43200$ - not $43200$. \n\nBest regards\nKostas\n\nsee here\nhttp://www.artofproblemsolving.com/wiki/index.php?title=2009 AMC_10B_Problems/Problem_28,\n  if $n,m$ are grater than $60$ or $1519$ respectively , we can divide into $8 sec$ period",
