      "sample_index": 3,
      "text": "To determine the number of ways to divide a day into \\( n \\) periods of \\( m \\) seconds, where \\( n \\) and \\( m \\) are positive integers, we need to find all pairs of positive integers \\((n, m)\\) that can satisfy this condition. Specifically, we are looking for all pairs \\((n, m)\\) where \\( n \\times m = 86,400 \\).\n\nFirst, we start with the prime factorization of 86,400:\n\\[\n86,400 = 86,400 \\div 2 = 43,200 \\div 2 = 21,600 \\div 2 = 10,800 \\div 2 = 5,400 \\div 2 = 2,700 \\div 2 = 1,350 \\div 2 = 675 \\times 5\n\\]\nThe number 675 can be further factorized:\n\\[\n675 = 3^4 \\times 5\n\\]\nThus, the prime factorization of 86,400 is:\n\\[\n86,400 = 2^7 \\times 3^4 \\times 5^2\n\\]\nEach prime factor in the factorization of 86,400 must be a factor of \\( n \\) and \\( m \\) in the pair. The number of divisors of a number is determined by adding 1 to each of the exponents in its prime factorization and then multiplying these results. Therefore, the number of divisors of \\( 86,400 = 2^7 \\times 3^4 \\times 5^2 \\) is:\n\\[\n(7+1)(4+1)(2+1) = 8 \\times 5 \\times 3 = 120\n\\]\nThis tells us that there are 120 pairs \\((n, m)\\) of positive integers that satisfy \\( n \\times m = 86,400 \\). Therefore, the number of ways to divide a day into \\( n \\) periods of \\( m \\) seconds is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{120}\n\\]",
